['Here's how you could create such method in C# using `HttpClient` and `async/await` to make HTTP requests concurrently. This solution uses Polly library for retry policies, which greatly simplifies the process of implementing these policies. Note that this is a simplified example and might need further refinements or adjustments based on your specific use case and requirements.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Polly;

public class Config 
{
    public TimeSpan Timeout { get; set; } = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10);
    public int MaxRetries { get; set; } = 3;
}

public static async Task<List<HttpResponseMessage>> ProcessRequestsAsync(IEnumerable<string> urls, Config config, HttpClient client) 
{
    var results = new List<HttpResponseMessage>();

    var policy = Policy.Handle<Exception>()
                      .WaitAndRetryAsync(config.MaxRetries, _ => config.Timeout);

    using (client) 
    {
        foreach (var url in urls) 
        {
            client.Timeout = config.Timeout;

            var resultTask = policy.ExecuteAsync(async () => 
            {
                return await client.GetAsync(url);
            });

            results.Add(await resultTask);
        }
    }

    return results;
}
```

To use this method, first create an instance of the `HttpClient` class and a configuration object with your preferred settings:

