['Here is the implementation of your function using C# and .NET XmlDocument:
```CSharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Xml;
using System.Linq;

public static List<XmlNode> GetElementsByName(string xml, string elementName) {
    if (String.IsNullOrEmpty(xml)) {
        return new List<XmlNode>();
    }

    XmlDocument doc = new XmlDocument() { PreserveWhitespace = true };
    try {
        doc.LoadXml(xml);
    } catch (Exception) {
        throw new FormatException("The input string is not a well-formed XML document.");
    }

    if (String.IsNullOrEmpty(elementName)) {
        return new List<XmlNode>();
    }

    elementName = elementName.Replace("'", "\""); // Use double quotes instead of single to avoid conflicts with XPath syntax
    string query = $"//*[local-name()='{elementName}']";

    XmlNodeList nodes;
    try {
        nodes = doc.DocumentElement.SelectNodes(query);
    } catch (Exception) {
        throw new ArgumentException("The provided xpath expression is not valid.");
    }

    if (nodes == null || nodes.Count == 0) {
        return new List<XmlNode>();
    } else if (nodes.Count > 1) {
        throw new InvalidOperationException($"Multiple elements found with the name '{elementName}'.");
    } else {
        return new List<XmlNode> { nodes[0] };
    }
}
```
This function loads the XML document into a `XmlDocument` and then constructs an XPath query to select all descendant elements of the root element with a local name equal to the provided element name. The resulting list is either empty, contains a single node, or throws an exception if it contains more than one node. 

Note that we replace any single quotes in `elementName` with double quotes to ensure that our XPath query remains well-formed. This helps prevent potential issues caused by malicious input strings where the attacker might try to close the string early and inject their own elements or attributes.
']

['```java
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.util.Arrays;
import org.apache.commons.codec.digest.DigestUtils;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

public class Main {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(Main.class);

    public static void main(String... args) {
        System.out.println(concatenateAndHashStrings("Hello", "world"));
    }

    private static String concatenateAndHashStrings(String... strings) {
        long start = System.currentTimeMillis();
        try {
            StringBuilder stringBuilder = new StringBuilder();
            int length = 0;

            for (String s : strings) {
                if ((length += s.length()) > 10_000) {
                    logger.error("Total length of input exceeded 10,000 characters");
                    return null;
                } else {
                    stringBuilder.append(s).append('-');
                }
            }

            // Remove trailing hyphen
            if (stringBuilder.length() > 0) {
                stringBuilder.setLength(stringBuilder.length() - 1);
            }

            String result = stringBuilder.toString();
            return DigestUtils.md5Hex(result.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
        } catch (Exception e) {
            logger.error("An exception occurred during MD5 hashing", e);
        } finally {
            long duration = System.currentTimeMillis() - start;
            logger.info("MD5 hash computation took {} ms", duration);
        }

        return null;
    }
}
```
This code uses the `org.apache.commons.codec.digest` library for MD5 hashing, and SLF4J for logging. The input strings are processed one by one, adding each to a StringBuilder until it exceeds 10,000 characters in total length. Once that threshold is reached, an error message is logged and the method returns null. If no exceptions occur during the computation of the MD5 hash, the duration of the computation is also logged.
']

