['This is quite an involved task, but I'll provide a simplified version of what you might be looking for. Please note that this example assumes you have access to common cryptographic hash functions and block ciphers in Java (such as `MessageDigest` for MD5/SHA hashes and `Cipher` for various block ciphers). 

```java
import java.security.*;
import javax.crypto.*;
import java.util.*;

class Algorithm {
    String name;
    MessageDigest messageDigest = null;
    Cipher cipher = null;

    public Algorithm(String name) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException, NoSuchPaddingException {
        this.name = name;
        try {
            if (Arrays.asList(Security.getProviders()).stream().anyMatch(p -> p.getName().contains("Sun"))){
                // Sun provider is for MD5/SHA hashes
                messageDigest = MessageDigest.getInstance(name);
            } else {
                cipher = Cipher.getInstance(name + "/ECB/PKCS5Padding");
            }
        } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException | NoSuchPaddingException e) {
            System.out.println("Error: " + name + " is not available.");
        }
    }
}

class LinkedListOfAlgorithms {
    Algorithm head = null;

    public void add(String name) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException, NoSuchPaddingException {
        if (head == null) {
            head = new Algorithm(name);
        } else {
            Algorithm current = head;
            while (current.next != null) {
                current = current.next;
            }
            current.next = new Algorithm(name);
        }
    }

    public void printActiveAlgorithms() {
        Algorithm current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            if (current.messageDigest != null || current.cipher != null){
                System.out.println(current.name + " is active.");
            } else {
                System.out.println(current.name + " is not active.");
            }
            current = current.next;
        }
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException, NoSuchPaddingException {
        LinkedListOfAlgorithms algorithms = new LinkedListOfAlgorithms();

        // Add your desired cryptographic algorithms here.
        algorithms.add("MD5");
        algorithms.add("AES");
        algorithms.add("DES");

        algorithms.printActiveAlgorithms();
    }
}
```
This code creates a simple linked list of `Algorithm` objects, each holding the name of an algorithm and either a `MessageDigest` or `Cipher` object if the algorithm is available on your system. It also has functionality to print which algorithms are active (i.e., have an associated `MessageDigest` or `Cipher`).
']

['Here is the code block that accomplishes what you're asking for in Rust using `hyper` crate:
```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
use hyper::{Client, Url};
use futures::future::FutureExt;

async fn make_request(
    url: &str, 
    disable_certificate_validation: bool, 
    headers: HashMap<&str, &str>,
    timeout: std::time::Duration,
    retries: u32,
) -> Result<hyper::Response, hyper::Error> {
    let connector = if disable_certificate_validation {
        // Use a client that skips certificate validation
        hyper::client::HttpsConnector::danger_accept();
    } else {
        // Default https connector
        hyper::client::HttpsConnector::new(4);
    };

    let client = Client::builder()
        .connector(hyper::client::HyperConnector::new(connector))
        .timeout(timeout)
        .build();

    for i in 0..retries {
        if let Ok(res) = client.get(Url::parse(url).unwrap()).headers(headers).await {
            return Ok(res);
        } else if i < retries - 1 {
            // Log that a retry is happening
            println_log(format![WARN: Retrying request to {}, due to error: {}", url, res.unwrap_err()]);
        }
    }

    Err(hyper::Error::Connect)
}
```
For the external logging service integration, please use appropriate log service's API or library in Rust to integrate with it. Also make sure to check if your project includes necessary permissions for using third-party libraries or services. 

Remember that Rust is a systems programming language which means there are no built-in logging capabilities like Python does with the `logging` module. You need to use an external library or service to integrate it with. 

Please note: This script assumes you have an existing logger function called `println_log()`, please replace this with your actual log function. And also don't forget to handle errors properly in a production environment.
']

