['Here's a simple implementation of the `RandomValueGenerator` class in C#:

```csharp
using System;

public class RandomValueGenerator
{
    private int minValue = 0;
    private int maxValue = 100;
    private readonly Random random = new Random();

    public RandomValueGenerator() { }

    public RandomValueGenerator(int min, int max)
    {
        if (min > max)
        {
            throw new ArgumentException("Minimum value cannot be greater than maximum value.");
        }

        this.minValue = min;
        this.maxValue = max + 1;
    }

    public float GetRandomFloat()
    {
        return (float)(random.NextDouble() * (maxValue - minValue) + minValue);
    }

    public int GetRandomInt()
    {
        return random.Next(minValue, maxValue);
    }

    public int GetRandomIntInRange(int rangeMin, int rangeMax)
    {
        if (rangeMin >= rangeMax)
        {
            throw new ArgumentException("Minimum value cannot be greater than or equal to maximum value.");
        }

        return random.Next(rangeMin, rangeMax);
    }

    public override string ToString()
    {
        return $"Range: [{minValue}, {maxValue})";
    }
}
```
In this implementation, the `RandomValueGenerator` class has a private field for the minimum and maximum values. The constructor takes these as arguments and initializes them to default values of 0 and 100, respectively. If provided with valid range values, it adjusts those fields accordingly. The class also includes methods to generate random floats and integers within the inclusive or exclusive ranges defined by `minValue` and `maxValue`. An override for the `ToString` method provides a human-readable representation of the current state of the object.
']

