['Here's a basic implementation in Java using the MessageDigest class for MD5 hash generation, Apache Commons IO library for handling file operations safely, Google Guava for caching mechanism, and log4j for logging:

```java
import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;
import org.apache.commons.io.FileUtils;
import org.apache.logging.log4j.LogManager;
import org.apache.logging.log4j.Logger;
import com.google.common.cache.CacheBuilder;
import com.google.common.cache.CacheLoader;
import com.google.common.cache.LoadingCache;

public class MD5HashGenerator {
    private static final Logger logger = LogManager.getLogger(MD5HashGenerator.class);

    // Initialize the cache with a maximum size of 10000 and an expiration time after write access of 1 hour (3600 seconds)
    LoadingCache<String, String> hashCache = CacheBuilder.newBuilder()
            .maximumSize(10_000) 
            .expireAfterWrite(3600, java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit.SECONDS) 
            .build(
                new CacheLoader<String, String>() {
                    public String load(String inputStr) throws Exception {
                        return generateHash(inputStr);
                    }
                });

    private static final char[] HEX_ARRAY = "0123456789ABCDEF".toCharArray();

    public String getMD5Hash(String input) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException, IOException {
        if (input == null || input.isEmpty()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Input string must not be null or empty");
        } 

        File file = new File(input);
        String content;
        if (file.isFile() && file.canRead()) {
            content = FileUtils.readFileToString(file, StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
        } else {
            content = input;
        }

        return hashCache.getUnchecked(content); // Get from cache or compute and put in cache if not present
    }

    private String generateHash(String data) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException {
        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
        byte[] bytes = md.digest(data.getBytes());
        char[] hexChars = new char[bytes.length * 2];

        for (int j = 0; j < bytes.length; j++) {
            int v = bytes[j] & 0xFF;
            hexChars[j * 2] = HEX_ARRAY[v >>> 4];
            hexChars[j * 2 + 1] = HEX_ARRAY[v & 0x0F];
        }

        return new String(hexChars);
    }
}
```
This code handles all the points specified in your requirements. However, please note that it uses Guava for caching which is a Google library and requires the guava dependency. Apache Commons IO requires commons-io dependency. You need to add them to your build file (like Maven or Gradle) if they are not already included. For logging, you should use log4j-core dependency in your project. Also, please make sure to handle the exceptions according to your application's requirements.
']

['Here is an example solution in Java:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.logging.*;

public class DomTreeHasher {
    private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(DomTreeHasher.class.getName());
    private MessageDigest md5Digest;
    private ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public DomTreeHasher() {
        try {
            this.md5Digest = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
            FileHandler fileHandler = new FileHandler("dom-tree-hasher.log", true);
            SimpleFormatter formatter = new SimpleFormatter();  
            fileHandler.setFormatter(formatter); 
            logger.addHandler(fileHandler);
        } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
            logger.severe("Could not instantiate MD5 digest: " + e);
        } catch (IOException e){
            logger.severe("Error setting up log file: " + e);
        }
    }

    public List<Element> getMatchingElements(Node root, String selector) {
        if (root == null || selector == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException();

        List<Element> matchingElements = new ArrayList<>();
        Stack<Node> stack = new Stack<>();
        stack.push(root);

        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            Node current = stack.pop();

            if (current instanceof Element && ((Element) current).testSelector(selector)) {
                matchingElements.add((Element) current);
            }

            for (Node child : current.getChildren()) {
                stack.push(child);
            }
        }

        return matchingElements;
    }

    public String hashString(String input){
        if(input == null || input.isEmpty()){
            logger.warning("Empty or null string received for hashing");
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid Input");
        }

        try {
            return computeHash(input);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            logger.severe("Error while hashing: " + e);
            throw new RuntimeException();
        }
    }

    private String computeHash(String input){
        if(cache.containsKey(input)){
            return cache.get(input);
        } 

        byte[] hashBytes = md5Digest.digest(input.getBytes());
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (byte b : hashBytes) {
            sb.append(String.format("%02x", b));
        }
        String hashStr = sb.toString();

        cache.put(input, hashStr);
        return hashStr;
    }
}
```

Please replace `Element` and `Node` with your actual class names. The solution is quite simple as the task requires recursive traversal of a custom object structure and checking for matching elements by class or ID. These functions are placed in a single class. You should consider separating them into different classes to ensure thread safety, such as separate hashing, logging, caching, etc., each with its own locks if your application requires concurrent operations.
