However, I can give you a basic example showing how you could start such a function in C using fork(), execvp(), wait() and kill(). 

Please note that this code does not include the functionality you specified. It just shows the basics of running a command in the background:

```c
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>

int run_in_background(char *command, char **argv) {
    pid_t pid = fork();

    if (pid == -1) {
        // Fork failed
        return 0;
    } else if (pid == 0) {
        // Child process. Execute command.

        execvp(command, argv);

        // If we reach here, the execution of the new program failed.
        exit(-1);
    } else {
        // Parent process.
        return 1;
    }
}
```
This function will fork a new process and in the child process, it will execute the given command with its arguments. In the parent process, the function returns immediately after forking the child. Please also note that this is a very simplified version of what you asked. Error handling, signal handling, logging etc., are missing here.

