["```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class DataEngine {
    private ExecutorService executor;
    private BlockingQueue<Runnable> queue;

    public DataEngine(int capacity) {
        this.queue = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(capacity);
        this.executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(0, Integer.MAX_VALUE, 60L, TimeUnit.SECONDS, queue);
    }

    public void submitTask(Runnable task) {
        executor.submit(task);
    }

    public void adjustThreads(int currentLoad, int maxCapacity) {
        if (currentLoad > maxCapacity) {
            ((ThreadPoolExecutor)executor).setCorePoolSize((int)(maxCapacity * 0.8));
            ((ThreadPoolExecutor)executor).setMaximumPoolSize(maxCapacity);
        } else {
            ((ThreadPoolExecutor)executor).setCorePoolSize(currentLoad / 2);
            ((ThreadPoolExecutor)executor).setMaximumPoolSize((int)(currentLoad * 1.5));
        }
    }

    public void shutDown() {
        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
```

In this code, we define a DataEngine class which takes an initial capacity as its constructor parameter. The capacity is used to create a bounded blocking queue, and the ThreadPoolExecutor uses it as its work queue. We submit tasks using `submitTask` method. 
The `adjustThreads` method dynamically adjusts the number of threads based on current load and maximum capacity. If the current load exceeds the maxCapacity, the core pool size is reduced to 80% of the maximum capacity and the maximum pool size is set to the maximum capacity. Otherwise, both are adjusted proportionally to the current load.
Finally, the `shutDown` method shuts down the executor service.

This example does not include data processing details (e.g., JSON parsing) which would require separate components or more complex code structures. Additionally, it's important to note that this code is a basic demonstration and doesn't incorporate all required features such as handling real-time analytics, integrating with third-party data sources, or implementing modular plugin architecture for transformation rules. To achieve such a comprehensive solution, you would need to implement more components including appropriate I/O handlers for different types of data formats, integration modules for APIs and external services, plugins system, error handling strategies, load balancing mechanisms, and much more.
