["```c
char *addSlash(const char *inputStr, size_t limit) {
    size_t length = strlen(inputStr);
    if (length > limit) {
        return NULL;
    } else {
        char *outputStr = malloc((limit + 2)*sizeof(char));
        if (!outputStr) {
            return NULL;  // Not enough memory to allocate.
        }
        strncpy(outputStr, inputStr, limit);
        outputStr[length] = '/';
        outputStr[length+1] = '\0';
        return outputStr;
    }
}
```
This C function takes a string and a size limit as input. If the length of the string is less than or equal to the limit, it appends a slash at the end of the string and returns it. Otherwise, it returns NULL. The returned string must be freed after use by calling free(). It's efficient because it doesn't scan the entire string when the limit has been reached and doesn't waste memory by creating an array that might not need to hold all of its elements.
"]

