static int logCount = 0;

void logMessage(int severity, const char *message) {
    if (severity > 5 || logCount >= 100) return; // limit to 100 logs/sec

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    fp = fopen(LOG_FILE, "a");
    if (!fp) {
        printf("Error opening log file.\n");
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
        return;
    }

    time_t now = time(NULL);

    if (now == lastLogTime && ftell(fp) > MAX_LOG_SIZE) {
        fclose(fp);
        fp = NULL; // memory leak prevention

        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
        return;
    } else if (now != lastLogTime) {
        logCount = 0;
        lastLogTime = now;
    }

    char buffer[BUFFER_SIZE]; 

    snprintf(buffer, BUFFER_SIZE, "Timestamp: %ld UserID: %d ThreadID: %lu Message: %s", 
             now, getuid(), pthread_self(), message);

    fputs(buffer, fp);
    logCount++;

    fclose(fp);
    fp = NULL; // memory leak prevention

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This code uses a mutex to ensure thread safety when writing to the log file. It checks if the current time is different from the last log time (indicating that 1 second has passed), and resets the counter if so. If the log file's size exceeds the maximum, it closes the file, reopens it on the next write operation, and returns without logging the message to maintain consistent formatting across all entries within a rotation period. It also checks if more than 100 logs have been written in the last second to prevent overwhelming downstream systems.

For the integration with an external logging framework or dynamic configuration at runtime through environment variables, you would need to use specific libraries or frameworks that support these features. The exact implementation would depend on what such a library or framework offers and how it is used.
']

