This example only includes the main parts and assumes that there's already an established endpoint list from which it can make requests. Please modify this according to your application needs. It's also important to note that you should handle exceptions properly in a real-world scenario.

```java
import okhttp3.*;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class RestClient {
    private final OkHttpClient client = new OkHttpClient();

    public void performRequests(List<String> endpoints) throws InterruptedException, ExecutionException {
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(endpoints.size());

        for (String endpoint : endpoints) {
            Future<Response> future = executor.submit(() -> 
                postRequest(endpoint));

            try {
                Response response = future.get();  // Blocks until the request is completed or failed.

                if (!response.isSuccessful()) throw new IOException("Unexpected code " + response);

                System.out.println("Request to endpoint: " + endpoint + " was successful.");
            } catch (IOException e) {
                System.err.println("Failed request to endpoint: " + endpoint);
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }

        executor.shutdown();
    }

    private Response postRequest(String url) throws IOException {
        RequestBody body = new FormBody.Builder()
                .add("key", "value") // Add your request parameters here
                .build();
        Request request = new Request.Builder()
                .url(url)
                .post(body)
                .build();
        return client.newCall(request).execute();
    }
}
``` 
