['Here's a basic implementation in C# using the `System.Xml` namespace. Note that this does not meet all your requirements as it lacks handling for large files, namespaces, complex XPath expressions etc.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Xml;

public class XmlParser
{
    private const int MaxDepth = 20; // Adjust this based on expected complexity of XPath queries

    public IEnumerable<string> Parse(string xml, string xpath)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(xml)) throw new ArgumentException("XML cannot be null or empty.", nameof(xml));
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(xpath)) throw new ArgumentException("XPath expression cannot be null or empty.", nameof(xpath));

        var xpNav = new XmlNamespaceManager(new NameTable());
        // Add namespace mappings here if needed

        var xmlDoc = new XmlDocument();
        try
        {
            xmlDoc.LoadXml(xml);
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Error parsing XML: {ex.Message}"); // Log detailed information about the error here
            return ArraySegment<string>.Empty; // Return a safe default value instead of crashing on malformed input
        }

        int depth = CountDepth(xpath);
        if (depth > MaxDepth) throw new ArgumentException("XPath expression is too complex.", nameof(xpath));

        var nodes = xmlDoc.SelectNodes(xpath, xpNav);
        if (nodes == null) return ArraySegment<string>.Empty; // If no nodes match the XPath query, return an empty collection

        foreach (XmlNode node in nodes)
        {
            yield return node.InnerText;
        }
    }

    private static int CountDepth(string xpath)
    {
        // This is a basic implementation and might not work for all XPath expressions, adjust according to your needs
        int depth = 0;
        foreach (var ch in xpath)
        {
            if (ch == '/') depth++;
        }
        return depth;
    }
}
```
This code uses an `XmlDocument` and a single XPath query to extract elements from the XML document. The code is designed for readability rather than performance, as it prioritizes safe error handling over maximum speed or memory efficiency. It's possible to enhance this code in several ways such as implementing caching, improving error messages etc., but these are significant changes and require more detailed requirements.
