['Here is the Java code that solves the problem using HashMap to keep track of anagrams. It also uses StopWatch from apache commons-lang3 library to calculate execution time. 

```java
import java.util.*;
import org.apache.commons.lang3.time.StopWatch;

public class AnagramProcessor {
    public static Map<String, List<List<String>>> groupAnagrams(String[] strings) {
        StopWatch stopwatch = new StopWatch();

        stopwatch.start();
        Map<String, List<List<String>>> anagramGroups = new HashMap<>();
        for (String s : strings) {
            if (s == null || s.trim().isEmpty()) continue;

            String[] words = s.split("\\s+");
            for (String word : words) {
                String sortedWord = sortLetters(word);

                if (!anagramGroups.containsKey(sortedWord)) {
                    anagramGroups.put(sortedWord, new ArrayList<>());
                }
                anagramGroups.get(sortedWord).add(Arrays.asList(words));
            }
        }

        stopwatch.stop();
        System.out.println("Execution time in milliseconds: " + stopwatch.getTime());
        return anagramGroups;
    }

    private static String sortLetters(String word) {
        char[] chars = word.toCharArray();
        Arrays.sort(chars);

        // Filter out non-alphabetic characters
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (char c : chars) {
            if (Character.isLetter(c)) {
                sb.append(c);
            }
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
}
```
In the code above, we iterate over each word in the array of strings, sort its letters and use it as a key for our HashMap to group anagrams together. We then store all words that have the same sorted sequence of letters into the List associated with this key. This approach works well even though we split on spaces which means multiple spaces between words will not matter in terms of detecting anagrams. For each word, sorting takes O(n log n) time where n is the number of characters in a string so overall it's a bit slower for large datasets but should be fine.
']

