Part 3 - Managing a high-throughput transaction processing system:

This task can be solved by creating a sliding window to track valid transactions based on their age, where the size of the window is dynamic.

```c
#include <pthread.h>
//...
typedef struct {
    double timestamp; // Transaction time
    int id;  // Unique identifier for transaction
    float value;  // Transaction value
} Transaction;

static pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
static LinkedList transactions;  // Custom linked list implementation.
static int maxWindowSize = 10;  // Maximum number of transactions in the window.
double startTime;
int validTransactionCount = 0;
float avgWeight, maxWeight = -FLT_MAX;
int maxWeightId = -1;

void addTransaction(Transaction *t) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    // Discard old transactions.
    while (!transactions.isEmpty() && transactions.getFirst()->timestamp < startTime - 5.0) {
        transactions.removeFirst();
    }

    if (transactions.size() >= maxWindowSize) {
        transactions.removeLast();
    }

    double sumWeights = 0;
    for(Transaction *t : transactions){
        float weight = abs(t->value - avgWeight);
        if(weight > maxWeight){
            maxWeight = weight;
            maxWeightId = t->id;
        }
        sumWeights += weight;
        validTransactionCount++;
    }

    avgWeight = (float)sumWeights/validTransactionCount;  // Update average weight.

    transactions.addFirst(t);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
In this code, we use a linked list and a sliding window to track recent transactions. When a new transaction comes in, we discard old transactions and add the new one to the front of our list. We then update metrics for valid transactions and their weights.
']

