["Creating a complete JavaScript utility function that dynamically loads, executes multiple files, handles errors, provides fallbacks, leverages Web Workers, monitors progress and caches results is quite complex task. This code below presents a simplified version of such a solution using modern JS features like Promises and Web Workers:

```javascript
class FileLoader {
  constructor() {
    this.cache = {};
  }

  loadFile(src) {
    if (this.cache[src]) {
      return Promise.resolve(this.cache[src]);
    }

    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      const worker = new Worker('/path/to/web-worker.js');

      worker.postMessage({ src });

      worker.onmessage = (e) => {
        this.cache[src] = e.data;

        resolve(e.data);
        worker.terminate();
      };

      worker.onerror = () => {
        reject(`Error loading file: ${src}`);
        worker.terminate();
      }
    });
  }

  loadFilesSequentially(sources) {
    return sources.reduce((promise, src) => 
      promise.then(() => this.loadFile(src)), Promise.resolve());
  }
}

class WebWorkerScriptLoader {
  constructor() {
    this.onmessage = null;
  }

  postMessage({src}) {
    import(src).then((module) => {
      if (this.onmessage) {
        this.onmessage({ data: module });
      }
    }, () => {
      if (this.onerror && typeof this.onerror === 'function') {
        this.onerror();
      }
    });
  }
}
```
The `FileLoader` class provides a method to load files sequentially, leveraging Web Workers for off-thread processing and caching loaded modules. The file loading process is wrapped in Promises to handle asynchronous behavior in a more readable way. If an error occurs during the loading process or execution of a file, it's caught and passed back to the promise.

