['This task involves multiple complexities, including memory allocation/deallocation optimization, concurrent access handling, thread-safety, large scale deployment considerations, and efficient resource utilization. However, without specific details of the network protocol PDU structures, unique MAC addresses, IP addresses and port pairing mechanism, it is challenging to provide a full implementation in C.

But let's try to give you a simple sketch of how such an implementation might look:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>

#define MAX_PORT 65535
#define MIN_PORT 0
#define IP_BUFFER_SIZE 16

typedef struct {
    char ip[IP_BUFFER_SIZE];
    int port;
} PduStructure;

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
PduStructure *buffer;
int bufferSize = 0;

void init(size_t size) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    if (size > bufferSize || buffer == NULL) {
        free(buffer);
        buffer = malloc(sizeof(PduStructure)*size);
        bufferSize = size;
    }
    memset(buffer, 0, sizeof(PduStructure)*bufferSize);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

int addEntry(const char* ip, int port) {
    if (port < MIN_PORT || port > MAX_PORT) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Invalid TCP/UDP port: %d\n", port);
        return -1;
    }

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    for (int i = 0; i < bufferSize; ++i) {
        if (strcmp(buffer[i].ip, ip) == 0 && buffer[i].port == port) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Duplicate IP/Port found\n");
            ++port;
            if (port > MAX_PORT) {
                fprintf(stderr, "Unable to allocate more ports\n");
                pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
                return -1;
            } else {
                --i; // try the same index again with incremented port.
                continue;
            }
        }
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < bufferSize; ++i) {
        if (buffer[i].port == 0) {
            strncpy(buffer[i].ip, ip, IP_BUFFER_SIZE - 1);
            buffer[i].port = port;
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
            return i;
        }
    }

    fprintf(stderr, "Buffer is full\n");
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    return -1;
}
```
This code assumes that each IP has a unique port. When adding an entry to the buffer, it first checks if there's a duplicate and increments the port until it finds a free one or reaches `MAX_PORT`. It then tries to find a free slot in the buffer to store the new entry. If all slots are taken, it logs an error message. This code does not validate MAC addresses or handle large scale deployments.

Also note that due to the nature of C and its memory management model, this code is not completely exception-safe, since `malloc` can fail causing a memory leak if `free` isn't called in all exceptional paths. You might want to use a different language with more advanced memory safety features if portability is a concern.

Lastly, this code does not provide monitoring of the memory usage or any performance optimizations such as buffer pooling for PDUs, caching of results, etc., which would be needed in a production-grade system handling large scale deployments.  These are complex topics that require careful design and extensive testing to ensure they don't introduce their own set of problems.
