["The following is a high-level implementation of your task using C programming language. It includes a structure definition for LTE_RRC_UE_CONTEXT_T, functions to add, update, and purge UE contexts, and a mechanism to handle concurrency with pthreads. Please note that this code should be further enhanced and fine tuned according to the specific requirements of your network node application.

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct {
    int MAC; // assuming MAC is just an integer
    int C_RNTI; 
    void* SIBs;
} LTE_RRC_UE_CONTEXT_T;

LTE_RRC_UE_CONTEXT_T* contexts = NULL;
int contextCount = 0;
pthread_mutex_t lock;

void addContext(int MAC, int C_RNTI, void* SIBs) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    // allocate memory for new context and increase the count
    contexts = (LTE_RRC_UE_CONTEXT_T*) realloc(contexts, sizeof(LTE_RRC_UE_CONTEXT_T) * (contextCount + 1));
    LTE_RRC_UE_CONTEXT_T newContext = {MAC, C_RNTI, SIBs};
    contexts[contextCount] = newContext;
    contextCount++;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void updateContext(int MAC, int C_RNTI, void* SIBs) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    for(int i = 0; i < contextCount; ++i) {
        if (contexts[i].MAC == MAC && contexts[i].C_RNTI == C_RNTI) {
            // update existing context with new data
            contexts[i].SIBs = SIBs;
            break;
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void purgeContext(int MAC, int C_RNTI) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    for(int i = 0; i < contextCount; ++i) {
        if (contexts[i].MAC == MAC && contexts[i].C_RNTI == C_RNTI) {
            // free memory associated with the context and shift other elements down
            free(contexts[i].SIBs);
            for(int j = i; j < contextCount - 1; ++j) {
                contexts[j] = contexts[j + 1];
            }
            contextCount--;
            break;
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```

This is just a very basic and high-level implementation, actual network node application would require much more complicated code which includes handling of memory allocation, concurrent operations, integration with other components etc. It's also important to consider that error checking and edge case handling in real-world scenarios would be even more complex.
"]

