    std::atomic<bool> stopFlag{false};

public:
    NetworkContextManager(unsigned seed = std::random_device{}()) : gen(seed), distr(0.0, 1.0) {}

    void generateRandomValues() {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mu); // For thread safety
        for (int n = 0; n < 10000; ++n) {
            std::cout << distr(gen) << ' ';  // Use the distribution
        }
    }

    void purgeUnusedContexts() {
        threads.emplace_back([this](){
            while (!stopFlag) {
                // Your logic for purging unused contexts goes here
                std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1));
            }
        });
    }

    ~NetworkContextManager() {
        stopFlag = true;
        for (auto& thread : threads) {
            if (thread.joinable()) {
                thread.join();
            }
        }
    }
};
```
This is a simplified version of what your class might look like. In the constructor, I have created an `std::mt19937` engine with a seed value that can be provided or defaults to `std::random_device{}()` which uses hardware randomness as source for random seed. 

In the `generateRandomValues()` method, I'm using `distr(gen)` to generate a new random number from a uniform distribution in the range [0,1). Note that `std::mt19937` is not thread safe by itself and requires synchronization if used across multiple threads.

In `purgeUnusedContexts()` method, I'm creating a separate thread that will periodically purge unused contexts. The flag `stopFlag` is atomic to prevent race conditions when stopping the thread. 

Finally, in the destructor, I stop all threads and join them before the class gets destroyed. This ensures no resources are left dangling if an exception occurs.
